---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleTools from '../components/ArticleTools.astro';
import '../styles/site.css';
import { downloadSeconds, formatDuration } from '../lib/convert';
import { DEV_USE_THRESHOLDS } from '../lib/devresult';
import { ACTIVITY_RATES, recommendSpeed } from '../lib/speed-needs';

const GB = 1_000_000_000;
const calc4k = ACTIVITY_RATES.streams4k.down;
const devStreaming = DEV_USE_THRESHOLDS.streaming4kDownMbps;

const netflix4kGbPerHour = 7;
const netflix4kAverageMbps = Math.round(((netflix4kGbPerHour * GB * 8) / 3600 / 1_000_000) * 10) / 10;

const emptyCounts = { streams4k: 0, streamsHd: 0, callsHd: 0, onlineGamers: 0, largeDownloads: 0, smartHomeDevices: 0 };
const streamRows = [1, 2, 3, 4].map((streams) => {
  const result = recommendSpeed({ ...emptyCounts, streams4k: streams });
  return {
    label: streams === 1 ? '1 TV in 4K' : `${streams} TVs in 4K at once`,
    raw: streams * calc4k,
    down: result.down,
    tier: result.tierDown,
  };
});
const busyHome = recommendSpeed({ ...emptyCounts, streams4k: 2, streamsHd: 1, callsHd: 1, smartHomeDevices: 10 });

const filmGb = netflix4kGbPerHour * 2;
const formatTime = (sizeBytes: number, mbps: number) => formatDuration(downloadSeconds(sizeBytes, mbps) ?? 0) ?? '';
const filmTimes = [25, 50, 100, 300, 500, 1000].map((mbps) => ({
  label: `${mbps.toLocaleString('en-US')} Mbps`,
  time: formatTime(filmGb * GB, mbps),
}));

const sources = {
  netflixSpeed: 'https://help.netflix.com/en/node/306',
  netflixData: 'https://help.netflix.com/en/node/87',
  netflixQuality: 'https://help.netflix.com/en/node/13444',
  netflixPlans: 'https://help.netflix.com/en/node/24926',
  netflixBuffering: 'https://help.netflix.com/en/node/230',
  youtube: 'https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/78358',
  fccGuide: 'https://www.fcc.gov/consumers/guides/broadband-speed-guide',
  disney: 'https://help.disneyplus.com/article/disneyplus-quality-speeds',
};

const questions = [
  {
    question: 'How many Mbps do I need for 4K streaming?',
    answer: `Netflix recommends 15 Mbps or higher for 4K, YouTube recommends a sustained 20 Mbps, and the FCC lists 25 Mbps for Ultra HD 4K video. Plan for about 25 Mbps for each TV that streams 4K at the same time.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Is 50 Mbps enough for 4K streaming?',
    answer: `Yes for one 4K TV, with room left for phones and browsing. Two 4K streams at once use most of a 50 Mbps plan, so a busy home with calls and downloads on top will feel tight.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Is 100 Mbps enough for two or three 4K TVs?',
    answer: `Usually, yes. Three 4K streams at the 20 Mbps our calculator uses come to ${3 * calc4k} Mbps, or ${streamRows[2].down} Mbps with 25% headroom, which still fits inside a 100 Mbps plan if the Wi-Fi is good.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'How much data does 4K streaming use?',
    answer: `Netflix says its Ultra HD 4K setting uses up to ${netflix4kGbPerHour} GB per hour, per device. A two-hour 4K film is therefore up to about ${filmGb} GB, which matters if your plan has a data cap.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does my 4K stream drop to HD when my speed test looks fine?',
    answer:
      'The most common causes are weak Wi-Fi at the TV, other devices using the line at the same time, or a device, cable or plan that does not support 4K. Netflix, for example, only plays 4K on its Premium plan and only when every device in the chain supports it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does 4K streaming need a fast upload speed?',
    answer:
      'No. Watching a stream is almost entirely download, so upload speed hardly matters for 4K playback. Upload matters for video calls, live streaming and backups instead.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: questions.map(({ question, answer }) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: answer },
  })),
};

const articleSchema = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Internet Speed for 4K Streaming: How Many Mbps Do You Need?',
  description:
    'Sourced guide to the internet speed 4K streaming needs on Netflix and YouTube, how many 4K TVs a plan can carry, how much data 4K uses and why 4K drops to HD.',
  author: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    '@id': 'https://guessthespeed.com/#author',
    name: 'NaveenKumar Namachivayam',
    url: 'https://qainsights.com',
  },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Guess the Speed',
    url: 'https://guessthespeed.com',
  },
  datePublished: '2026-10-28',
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://guessthespeed.com/internet-speed-for-4k-streaming/',
};
---

<Base
  title="Internet Speed for 4K Streaming: How Many Mbps? (2026)"
  description="How much internet speed 4K streaming needs in 2026: Netflix and YouTube figures, Mbps for several 4K TVs, data use per hour and why 4K drops to HD."
  breadcrumb="4K streaming speed"
  schema={[articleSchema, faqSchema]}
>
  <main class="wrap content-page">
    <a class="round-banner" href="/speed-school/">Speed School</a>
    <h1>Internet Speed for 4K Streaming: How Many Mbps Do You Need?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Short answer: one 4K stream needs about <strong>15 to 25 Mbps</strong> of steady download speed. Netflix recommends
      <strong>15 Mbps</strong>, YouTube <strong>20 Mbps</strong> and the FCC <strong>25 Mbps</strong>, so plan for roughly 25 Mbps for every TV
      that plays 4K at the same time, plus room for everything else in the house.
    </p>
    <ArticleTools path="/internet-speed-for-4k-streaming/" />
    <article class="content-card stk">
      <p>
        Friday night in our house goes like this. The film starts in glorious 4K, someone upstairs starts a game download, and five minutes later
        the picture goes soft and the kids shout, "Appa, the TV went blurry!" Nobody touched the TV. The stream simply decided the connection could
        not keep up and dropped the quality. In this article, let us look at how much speed 4K really needs, where those numbers come from, how
        many 4K TVs your plan can carry, and what to check when 4K quietly turns into HD.
      </p>

      <h2>How much internet speed do you need for 4K streaming?</h2>
      <p>
        The streaming services publish their own figures, and they do not all agree. Here is what each source says today, next to the numbers this
        site uses in its own tools.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Published download speeds for 4K streaming" tabindex="0">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <caption>Download speed recommended for one 4K stream</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">4K figure</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href={sources.netflixSpeed} rel="noopener nofollow">Netflix</a></th><td>15 Mbps or higher</td><td>Ultra HD (4K); 5 Mbps for 1080p, 3 Mbps for 720p</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href={sources.youtube} rel="noopener nofollow">YouTube</a></th><td>20 Mbps sustained</td><td>4K UHD; 5 Mbps for 1080p, 2.5 Mbps for 720p</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href={sources.fccGuide} rel="noopener nofollow">FCC Broadband Speed Guide</a></th><td>25 Mbps</td><td>Minimum download for streaming Ultra HD 4K video</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/how-much-internet-speed-do-i-need/">Our household calculator</a></th><td>{calc4k} Mbps</td><td>Per 4K stream, before 25% headroom</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/?dev=1">Dev mode</a></th><td>{devStreaming} Mbps</td><td>Download needed for the 4K streaming tick</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        So which one is right? All of them, for what they describe. Netflix and YouTube are telling you what their own players need. The FCC number
        is a general guide for any service, and it sits a little higher to leave some slack. Our calculator uses YouTube's 20 Mbps as the planning
        allowance per stream and then adds headroom on top, and Dev mode uses the FCC's 25 Mbps as its pass mark.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also looked for Disney+. Its <a href={sources.disney} rel="noopener nofollow">help centre page on video quality and speeds</a> would not
        load its content while I was writing this, so I am not quoting a Disney+ number here. If you watch mostly Disney+, open that page and check
        its current 4K figure yourself.
      </p>

      <h2>Why is 4K so much hungrier than HD?</h2>
      <p>
        A 4K picture has four times as many pixels as a 1080p picture, and the stream has to deliver all of them, every second, without a pause.
        Netflix asks for 15 Mbps for 4K against 5 Mbps for 1080p, and YouTube asks for 20 Mbps against 5 Mbps. That is three to four times the
        bandwidth for the same film.
      </p>
      <p>
        I spent years in performance testing before moving into a developer role, and a video stream behaves a lot like a constant-load test. It is
        not a burst that finishes quickly, like loading a web page. It is a steady demand for throughput that lasts the whole film. If the
        connection dips below that rate for long enough, the player has two choices: pause and buffer, or drop the quality. Most services choose to
        drop the quality, which is why the picture goes soft rather than stopping.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is also why YouTube says <em>sustained</em> speed. A speed test that peaks at 300 Mbps for ten seconds does not help if the Wi-Fi in the
        living room sags to 12 Mbps every time someone opens the microwave.
      </p>

      <h2>How many 4K TVs can my internet plan handle?</h2>
      <p>
        Streams add up. Two people watching different 4K shows on two TVs need twice the bandwidth, and the line is shared with phones, laptops and
        consoles too. This table uses the same code as our <a href="/how-much-internet-speed-do-i-need/">household speed calculator</a>: {calc4k} Mbps per 4K stream, plus 25% headroom, rounded up to a common plan tier.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Download speed needed for several 4K streams" tabindex="0">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <caption>4K streams at the same time and the plan to look for</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Streams</th><th scope="col">Stream total</th><th scope="col">With 25% headroom</th><th scope="col">Plan tier</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {streamRows.map((row) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{row.label}</th><td>{row.raw} Mbps</td><td>{row.down} Mbps</td><td>{row.tier} Mbps</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        These are streams only. A more realistic family evening, with two 4K TVs, one HD tablet, one video call and ten connected devices, comes to
        about {busyHome.down} Mbps down in the calculator, which rounds to a {busyHome.tierDown} Mbps plan. If a big game download starts at the same
        time, it will happily take whatever is left, and that is often the moment the TV drops to HD. I explain why that happens in <a href="/why-is-my-internet-slow-when-downloading/">why your internet is slow when someone is downloading</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How much data does 4K streaming use?</h2>
      <p>
        If your plan has a monthly data cap, 4K is where you will feel it. Netflix's <a href={sources.netflixData} rel="noopener nofollow">data usage
        page</a> says its High setting uses up to {netflix4kGbPerHour} GB per hour, per device, for Ultra HD 4K, compared with up to 3 GB for HD and
        1 GB for standard definition.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is a nice cross-check. {netflix4kGbPerHour} GB in one hour works out to an average of about {netflix4kAverageMbps} Mbps, which lines up with
        Netflix's 15 Mbps recommendation. The speed figure and the data figure are two views of the same stream. Two hours of 4K is up to about {filmGb} GB, and three people each watching two hours of 4K a night for a month is well over a terabyte.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same size also tells you how long it takes to download a 4K film to watch offline, where the service allows it. Times below are ideal,
        calculated with the same code as our <a href="/mbps-to-mbs/">Mbps to MB/s converter</a>. Real downloads are usually a bit slower.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Ideal download time for a two-hour 4K film" tabindex="0">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <caption>Ideal time to download about {filmGb} GB (two hours of 4K at {netflix4kGbPerHour} GB per hour)</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Plan speed</th><th scope="col">Download time</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {filmTimes.map((row) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{row.label}</th><td>{row.time}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Why does my 4K stream drop to HD?</h2>
      <p>
        Speed is only one of the things that has to line up. When 4K will not play, or keeps falling back to HD, work through these in order.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Your plan with the service.</strong> On Netflix, 4K (Ultra HD) and HDR are part of the <a href={sources.netflixPlans} rel="noopener nofollow">Premium plan</a>. On a lower plan you will never see 4K, however fast your line is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every device in the chain.</strong> Netflix's <a href={sources.netflixQuality} rel="noopener nofollow">video quality guide</a> says
          that if you watch through a TV, a streaming stick and an audio receiver, all three have to support 4K or HDR. The HDMI cables and the ports
          they plug into matter too.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The title itself.</strong> Not every film or show is available in 4K. Netflix shows a 4K label on the details page when the title
          will play in 4K on your device.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The quality setting.</strong> Netflix says its data usage setting needs to be High or Auto for 4K. If someone set it to Medium to save
          data, 4K is off.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The Wi-Fi at the TV.</strong> This is the one I see most often. The TV is usually the furthest device from the router, often behind a
          wall or inside a cabinet.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Is Wi-Fi fast enough for 4K, or should I use a cable?</h2>
      <p>
        Wi-Fi is often fine for 4K, but it is the weakest link in most living rooms. Netflix's own <a href={sources.netflixBuffering} rel="noopener nofollow">buffering help page</a> suggests moving the router and the device closer
        together, ideally into the same room, moving the router away from other wireless devices and appliances, and keeping it in an open space off
        the floor, such as on a desk or bookshelf.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your TV or streaming box has an Ethernet port, a cable takes Wi-Fi out of the question entirely. It is the cheapest upgrade most homes can
        make for 4K, and it frees up airtime for everyone else's phones too. The simplest way to know whether you need it is to measure at the TV
        itself, which brings me to the next part.
      </p>

      <h2>How do I check if my connection can handle 4K?</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Test where you watch.</strong> Run the test on a phone or laptop sitting next to the TV, not next to the router. Netflix also has a
          built-in check under Get Help, then Check your Network, on many TVs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Test when you watch.</strong> Evening, with the rest of the family online, is the result that matters for Friday night.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read the download number against the table above.</strong> <a href="/?dev=1">Dev mode</a> on this site shows your download, upload
          and ping, and gives a 4K streaming tick when download is {devStreaming} Mbps or more.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Count the TVs.</strong> If several screens stream at once, use the <a href="/how-much-internet-speed-do-i-need/">household speed
          calculator</a> to add them up with headroom.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compare with your plan.</strong> If the result is far below what you pay for, even close to the router, follow the
          <a href="/am-i-getting-the-speed-i-pay-for/">plan speed check</a> before calling your provider.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        One honest caveat. Our test measures to a nearby Cloudflare server. It tells you how healthy your home line and Wi-Fi are, which is usually
        where 4K problems start. It does not prove that Netflix, YouTube or Disney+ will deliver 4K to you, because their video comes from their own
        servers over their own routes, and the service decides the quality in the end. For general targets beyond streaming, see <a href="/what-is-a-good-internet-speed/">what is a good internet speed</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Want to make it fun? Before the film starts, get everyone to <a href="/">guess the speed</a> at the TV, then run the test. Whoever is closest
        picks the film. In my house, the kids win that one every time.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {questions.map(({ question, answer }) => (
          <section>
            <h3>{question}</h3>
            <p>{answer}</p>
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        Streaming games instead of films? Read <a href="/internet-speed-for-gaming/">how much internet speed you need for gaming</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="story-cta">
        <a class="go" href="/">Start a game</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
